WEBVTT

00:00.680 --> 00:03.420
A rectangular storage
container with an open top

00:03.420 --> 00:06.790
needs to have a volume
of 10 cubic meters.

00:06.790 --> 00:11.290
The length of its base
is twice the width.

00:11.290 --> 00:14.840
Material for the base
costs $10 per square meter.

00:14.840 --> 00:17.950
Material for the sides
costs $6 per square meter.

00:17.950 --> 00:21.440
Find the cost of the material
for the cheapest container.

00:21.440 --> 00:24.570
So let's draw this
open storage container,

00:24.570 --> 00:27.040
this open rectangular
storage container.

00:27.040 --> 00:28.720
So it's going to
have an open top.

00:28.720 --> 00:33.190
So let me draw its open
top as good as I can.

00:33.190 --> 00:35.250
So it's going to
have an open top.

00:35.250 --> 00:37.310
That's the top of my container.

00:37.310 --> 00:38.945
And then let me draw the sides.

00:43.130 --> 00:45.330
Just like that.

00:45.330 --> 00:47.780
So it might look
something like that.

00:47.780 --> 00:50.960
And then I could draw--
and since it's open top,

00:50.960 --> 00:53.890
I can see through, I could see
the inside of the container

00:53.890 --> 00:54.410
as well.

00:54.410 --> 00:59.774
So the container would
look something like that.

00:59.774 --> 01:00.940
And so what do they tell us?

01:00.940 --> 01:03.935
They tell us that the volume
needs to be 10 cubic meters.

01:03.935 --> 01:05.110
So let me write that down.

01:05.110 --> 01:09.360
The volume needs to be
equal to 10 meters cubed.

01:09.360 --> 01:13.040
The length of its base
is twice the width.

01:13.040 --> 01:17.090
So the length, let's
call the width x,

01:17.090 --> 01:18.910
so the length is going
to be twice that.

01:18.910 --> 01:20.350
It's going to be 2x.

01:20.350 --> 01:22.320
That's what they tell
us right over here.

01:22.320 --> 01:24.110
They tell us the
material for the base

01:24.110 --> 01:28.490
costs $10 per square meter.

01:28.490 --> 01:32.530
So this area right
over here-- [INAUDIBLE]

01:32.530 --> 01:35.560
if I was transparent I could
continue to draw it down here.

01:35.560 --> 01:38.010
But this right over
here, that material

01:38.010 --> 01:40.150
costs $10 per square meter.

01:40.150 --> 01:45.324
Let me label that
$10 per square meter.

01:45.324 --> 01:46.990
And then they say
material for the sides

01:46.990 --> 01:49.830
costs $6 per square meter.

01:49.830 --> 01:57.680
So the material over here
costs $6 per meter squared.

01:57.680 --> 02:03.310
So let's see if we can
come up with a value

02:03.310 --> 02:08.550
or how much this box would cost
to make as a function of x.

02:08.550 --> 02:10.661
But x only gives us the
dimensions of the base.

02:10.661 --> 02:12.160
We also need a
dimension for height.

02:12.160 --> 02:15.320
So it'll be a function
of x and height for now.

02:15.320 --> 02:18.790
So let's write h as the
height right over here.

02:18.790 --> 02:21.410
So what is the cost of
this container going to be?

02:21.410 --> 02:27.090
So the cost is going to be
equal to the cost of the base.

02:27.090 --> 02:31.750
Well, the cost of the base
is going to be $10 times--

02:31.750 --> 02:33.290
I'll just write 10.

02:33.290 --> 02:36.634
This is going to be 10
times the area of the base.

02:36.634 --> 02:38.050
Well, what's the
area of the base?

02:38.050 --> 02:40.133
Well, it's going to be the
width times the length.

02:40.133 --> 02:44.380
So 10 times x times 2x.

02:44.380 --> 02:46.285
That is the cost of base.

02:50.400 --> 02:52.872
And now what's going to
be the cost of the sides?

02:52.872 --> 02:54.330
Well, the different
sides are going

02:54.330 --> 02:55.690
to have different dimensions.

02:55.690 --> 03:00.040
You have this side
over here and this side

03:00.040 --> 03:03.750
over here, which have
the same dimension.

03:03.750 --> 03:06.370
They both have an
area of x times h.

03:06.370 --> 03:09.110
You have x times h.

03:09.110 --> 03:12.490
And then our material
is $6 per square meter.

03:12.490 --> 03:16.670
So it's 6 times x times h
would be the cost of one

03:16.670 --> 03:18.540
of these side panels.

03:18.540 --> 03:20.920
So for two of them we
have to multiply by 2.

03:20.920 --> 03:24.080
So plus 2 times 6 times h.

03:24.080 --> 03:25.890
And then we have
these two side panels.

03:25.890 --> 03:29.070
We have this side
panel right over here

03:29.070 --> 03:32.340
and we have this side
panel right over here.

03:32.340 --> 03:36.760
The area of each of them
is going to be 2x times h.

03:36.760 --> 03:41.380
So it's going to be 2x times h.

03:41.380 --> 03:43.970
The cost of the material
is going to be 6.

03:43.970 --> 03:45.430
So the cost of one
of the panels is

03:45.430 --> 03:49.320
going to be $6 per square
meters times 2xh meters squared.

03:49.320 --> 03:50.760
But we have two of these panels.

03:50.760 --> 03:53.060
One panel and two panels.

03:53.060 --> 03:55.390
So we have to multiply by 2.

03:55.390 --> 03:58.120
And so we will get-- so
this is right over here,

03:58.120 --> 03:59.410
this is the cost of the sides.

04:03.652 --> 04:05.360
And so let's see if
we can simplify this.

04:05.360 --> 04:06.640
And I'll write it all
in a neutral color.

04:06.640 --> 04:08.360
So this is going
to be equal to 10.

04:08.360 --> 04:08.860
Let's see.

04:08.860 --> 04:10.690
10 times 2 is 20.

04:10.690 --> 04:13.660
x times x is x squared.

04:13.660 --> 04:16.620
And then you have
2 times 6 times xh.

04:16.620 --> 04:22.206
So this is going
to be plus 12xh.

04:22.206 --> 04:24.080
And then this is going
to be 2 times 6, which

04:24.080 --> 04:30.560
is 12 times 2 is 24xh plus 24xh.

04:30.560 --> 04:39.730
So this is going to be equal
to 20x squared plus 36xh.

04:39.730 --> 04:41.339
So this is going to be my cost.

04:41.339 --> 04:42.880
But I'm not ready
to optimize it yet.

04:42.880 --> 04:45.000
We don't know how to optimize
with respect to two variables.

04:45.000 --> 04:47.416
We only know how to optimize
with respect to one variable,

04:47.416 --> 04:50.460
and maybe I'll say let's
optimize with respect to x.

04:50.460 --> 04:52.730
But if we want to optimize
with respect to x,

04:52.730 --> 04:56.360
we have to express h
as a function of x.

04:56.360 --> 04:57.650
So how can we do that?

04:57.650 --> 05:01.030
How can we express h
as a function of x?

05:01.030 --> 05:05.310
Well, we know that the volume
has to be 10 cubic meters.

05:05.310 --> 05:13.300
So we know that x, the width
times the length times 2x times

05:13.300 --> 05:17.010
the height times h
needs to be equal to 10.

05:17.010 --> 05:19.880
Or another way of saying
that, this tells us

05:19.880 --> 05:24.210
that 2x squared h,
2x squared times h

05:24.210 --> 05:25.700
needs to be equal to 10.

05:25.700 --> 05:27.430
And so if we want h
as a function of x,

05:27.430 --> 05:30.290
we just divide both
sides by 2x squared.

05:30.290 --> 05:37.720
And we get h is equal
to 10 over 2x squared.

05:37.720 --> 05:44.550
Or we could say that h is
equal to 5 over x squared.

05:44.550 --> 05:47.430
And then we can substitute
back right over here.

05:47.430 --> 05:50.930
h is equal to 5 over x squared.

05:50.930 --> 05:55.290
So all of this business is
going to be equal to 20 times

05:55.290 --> 06:00.950
x squared plus 36 times
x times 5 over x squared.

06:05.490 --> 06:12.810
So our cost as a function
of x is going to be 20x

06:12.810 --> 06:15.000
squared 36 times 5.

06:15.000 --> 06:17.130
Let's see, 30
times 5 is 150 plus

06:17.130 --> 06:19.340
another 30 is going to be 180.

06:19.340 --> 06:22.170
So it's going to be plus
180 times, let's see,

06:22.170 --> 06:27.730
x times x to the negative
2, 180x to the negative x

06:27.730 --> 06:30.150
to the negative 1 power.

06:30.150 --> 06:32.340
So we finally have cost
as a function of x.

06:32.340 --> 06:34.700
Now we're ready to optimize.

06:34.700 --> 06:36.650
To optimize, we just
have to figure out

06:36.650 --> 06:38.784
what are the
critical points here

06:38.784 --> 06:40.325
and whether those
critical points are

06:40.325 --> 06:42.490
a minimum or a maximum value.

06:42.490 --> 06:44.972
So let's see what we can do.

06:44.972 --> 06:47.430
So to find a critical point,
we take the derivative, figure

06:47.430 --> 06:50.357
out where the derivative
is undefined or equal to 0,

06:50.357 --> 06:52.190
and those are our
candidate critical points.

06:52.190 --> 06:54.060
And then from the
critical points

06:54.060 --> 06:57.040
we find, they might be
minimum or maximum values.

06:57.040 --> 07:00.980
So the derivative of c of
our cost with respect to x

07:00.980 --> 07:09.310
is going to be equal to 40
times x minus 180 times x

07:09.310 --> 07:11.450
to the negative 2 power.

07:11.450 --> 07:16.730
Now, this seems-- well
it's defined for all x

07:16.730 --> 07:18.910
except for x equaling 0.

07:18.910 --> 07:21.157
But x equaling 0 is
not interesting to us

07:21.157 --> 07:22.740
as a critical point
because then we're

07:22.740 --> 07:23.864
going to have a degenerate.

07:23.864 --> 07:25.454
This is going to
have no base at all.

07:25.454 --> 07:27.620
So we don't want to worry
about that critical point.

07:27.620 --> 07:30.009
We would have no volume at
all, so it would not work out.

07:30.009 --> 07:31.800
And actually, if x
equals 0 then our height

07:31.800 --> 07:34.260
is undefined as well.

07:34.260 --> 07:36.780
So this was defined
for everything else,

07:36.780 --> 07:39.410
for anything other
than x equals 0.

07:39.410 --> 07:41.520
So let's see when
this derivative is

07:41.520 --> 07:46.590
equal to 0 in our search for
our potential critical points.

07:46.590 --> 07:48.900
So when does-- I'll
do it over here.

07:48.900 --> 07:57.760
When does 40x minus 180x
to the negative 2 equal 0?

07:57.760 --> 08:00.530
Well, we could add the 180x to
the negative 2 to both sides.

08:00.530 --> 08:04.310
We get 40x is equal to 180.

08:04.310 --> 08:10.010
And I could write it
as 180 over x squared.

08:10.010 --> 08:10.740
Now let's see.

08:10.740 --> 08:14.940
We could multiply both sides
of this equation by x squared

08:14.940 --> 08:21.430
and we would get 40x to
the third is equal to 180.

08:21.430 --> 08:23.680
Divide both sides by 40.

08:23.680 --> 08:29.040
You get x to the third
is equal to 180 over 40,

08:29.040 --> 08:32.400
which is the same
thing as 18 over 4,

08:32.400 --> 08:35.350
which is the same
thing as 9 over 2.

08:35.350 --> 08:36.980
And so if we want
to solve for x,

08:36.980 --> 08:41.730
we get that x is equal
to a critical point.

08:41.730 --> 08:47.370
We get a critical point of x is
equal to 9/2 to the 1/3 power,

08:47.370 --> 08:48.689
the cube root of 9/2.

08:48.689 --> 08:49.230
So let's see.

08:49.230 --> 08:52.600
Let's get an approximate
value for what that is.

08:52.600 --> 08:58.150
So if we take 9/2,
9 divided by 2--

08:58.150 --> 08:59.820
I guess you could
call that 4.5--

08:59.820 --> 09:02.670
and we want to raise
it to the 1/3 power.

09:02.670 --> 09:06.860
To the 1/3 power we get 1.65.

09:06.860 --> 09:12.925
So it's approximately equal
to 1.65 as our critical point.

09:12.925 --> 09:14.300
Now the way the
problem is asked,

09:14.300 --> 09:17.280
we're only getting one
legitimate critical point here.

09:17.280 --> 09:19.840
So that's probably going
to be the x at which we

09:19.840 --> 09:23.000
achieve a minimum value.

09:23.000 --> 09:25.930
But let's use our second
derivative test just in case

09:25.930 --> 09:27.500
to make sure that
we're definitely

09:27.500 --> 09:29.830
concave upwards over here,
in which case, this will

09:29.830 --> 09:32.160
definitely be the
x value at which we

09:32.160 --> 09:33.960
achieve a minimum value.

09:33.960 --> 09:35.615
So the second derivative.

09:35.615 --> 09:36.740
I'll do it right over here.

09:36.740 --> 09:39.060
The second derivative
of our cost function

09:39.060 --> 09:40.850
is just the derivative
of this, which

09:40.850 --> 09:49.770
is going to be equal to 40 minus
180 times negative 2, which

09:49.770 --> 09:51.660
is negative 360.

09:51.660 --> 09:58.960
So it's going to be plus
360 over x to the 3.

09:58.960 --> 10:01.970
The derivative of this is
negative 2 times negative 180,

10:01.970 --> 10:04.460
which is positive 360x to
the negative 3 power, which

10:04.460 --> 10:06.630
is exactly this right over here.

10:06.630 --> 10:10.200
So when x is equal to 1.65,
this is going to be positive.

10:10.200 --> 10:11.740
This is going to be positive.

10:11.740 --> 10:15.620
So let me write this down.
c prime prime of 1.65

10:15.620 --> 10:18.050
is definitely greater than 0.

10:18.050 --> 10:22.320
So we're definitely concave
upwards when x is 1.65.

10:22.320 --> 10:25.500
Concave upwards, which
means that our graph is

10:25.500 --> 10:27.260
going to look
something like this.

10:27.260 --> 10:28.680
And so where the
derivative equal

10:28.680 --> 10:32.540
to 0, which is right over there,
we are at a minimum point.

10:32.540 --> 10:35.424
We are minimizing our cost.

10:35.424 --> 10:37.590
And so if we go back to the
question, the only thing

10:37.590 --> 10:39.750
that we have to do now--
We know the x value

10:39.750 --> 10:41.040
that minimizes our cost.

10:41.040 --> 10:44.070
We now have to find the cost of
the material for the cheapest

10:44.070 --> 10:44.920
container.

10:44.920 --> 10:48.456
So we just have to figure
out what our cost is.

10:48.456 --> 10:49.830
And we already
know what our cost

10:49.830 --> 10:51.460
is as a function
of x, so we just

10:51.460 --> 10:55.240
have to put 1.65
into this equation.

10:55.240 --> 10:57.270
Evaluate the function at 1.65.

10:57.270 --> 10:59.690
So let's do that.

10:59.690 --> 11:05.520
Our cost is going to be
equal to 20 times 1.65.

11:05.520 --> 11:07.020
I should say
approximately equal to,

11:07.020 --> 11:08.436
because I'm using
an approximation

11:08.436 --> 11:09.860
of this original value.

11:09.860 --> 11:18.870
1.65 squared plus 180.

11:18.870 --> 11:20.681
I could say divided by 1.65.

11:20.681 --> 11:22.180
That's the same
thing as multiplying

11:22.180 --> 11:24.140
by 1.65 to the negative 1.

11:24.140 --> 11:33.520
So divided by 1.65,
which is equal to 163.

11:33.520 --> 11:37.370
I'll just say $163.5.

11:37.370 --> 11:38.670
So it's approximately.

11:38.670 --> 11:42.100
So the cost-- let me
do this in a new color.

11:42.100 --> 11:44.420
We deserve a drum roll now.

11:44.420 --> 11:57.770
The cost when x is 1.65 is
approximately equal to $163.54.

11:57.770 --> 12:05.300
So $163.54, which is
quite an expensive box.

12:05.300 --> 12:07.610
So this is kind of expensive.

12:07.610 --> 12:10.370
This is fairly
expensive material here.

12:10.370 --> 12:11.820
Although it's a
fairly large box.

12:11.820 --> 12:16.410
1.65 meters in
width, and it's going

12:16.410 --> 12:18.110
to be twice that in length.

12:18.110 --> 12:20.830
And then you could figure out
what its height is going to be.

12:20.830 --> 12:22.620
Although it's not
going to be too tall.

12:22.620 --> 12:24.567
5 divided by 1.65 squared.

12:24.567 --> 12:27.150
I don't know, it'll be roughly
a little under two meters tall.

12:27.150 --> 12:30.485
So it actually is quite
a large box made out

12:30.485 --> 12:32.310
of quite expensive material.

12:32.310 --> 12:37.240
The minimum cost to make this
box is going to be $163.54.
